---
import PublicationLayout from '../../layouts/PublicationLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'availability', label: 'What is available' },
  { id: 'independent-checking', label: 'What anyone can check' },
  { id: 'release-scope', label: 'Release 1 scope' },
  { id: 'signed-release', label: 'Signed release artifacts' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'What this release does not establish' },
];
---

<PublicationLayout
  category="News"
  type="Product"
  title="nomue Protocol Release 1 Public Draft is now available"
  description="The first public draft of the nomue Protocol is available with a machine-readable Record specification, local verification, public fixtures, and signed release artifacts."
  published="August 24, 2026"
  status="Public Draft — experimental"
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="availability">
    <h2>What is available</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-protocol" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nomue Protocol Release 1</a>
      is now publicly available. It defines public structures and verification rules for
      nomue Records—machine-readable evidence objects that connect scientific-analysis
      declarations, analytical decisions, inputs, results, and other finalized evidence
      so that supported properties can be checked independently.
    </p>
    <p>
      Release 1 includes the public specification, schemas, registries, conformance
      materials, a bundled reference verifier, and signed release artifacts. The
      separately packaged <a href="https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-verifier" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nomue verifier</a>
      is also pinned to the published Release 1 identities.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-protocol/releases/tag/release-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Release 1 on GitHub</a>
      or <a href="https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-verifier" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">run the nomue verifier</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="independent-checking">
    <h2>What anyone can check</h2>
    <p>
      After its dependencies are installed, the verifier runs locally and does not need a
      Licklider server to verify a Record. For the exact Release 1 support target, it can
      check Record structure and content integrity, whether the declared research design
      falls inside the supported profile, whether the Welch calculation is computable,
      and whether stored Welch results match a fresh calculation under the registered
      rules.
    </p>
    <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-verifier.git
cd nomue-verifier
npm install

npm exec -- nomue verify records/valid.json --format json
npm exec -- nomue verify records/invalid-result-mismatch.json --format json</code></pre>
    <p>
      The first public fixture is expected to pass every check supported by this version.
      The second deliberately contains a reported-result mismatch and is expected to fail
      the relevant check.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="release-scope">
    <h2>Release 1 scope</h2>
    <p>
      Release 1 is an experimental Public Draft, not a Stable 1.0 compatibility promise.
      Its sole public Record interpretation support target is
      <code>urn:nomue:bundle:itgc-guarantee:0.2.1-draft.1</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      That target covers independent two-group continuous outcomes using the two-sided
      Welch two-sample t procedure, including the unstandardized arithmetic mean
      difference, standard error, and two-sided 95% Welch-Satterthwaite confidence
      interval. Other statistical procedures are outside the Release 1 public support
      boundary.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="signed-release">
    <h2>Signed release artifacts</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-protocol/releases/tag/release-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Release 1 page</a>
      includes a separately attached signed source archive, release checksums, a Protocol
      snapshot manifest, detached signatures, and the public release key. These artifacts
      make the published release evidence independently inspectable.
    </p>
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        GitHub’s automatically generated <strong>Source code</strong> archives are not the
        signed Release 1 source artifact. Signature verification should use the separately
        attached <code>source-archive.tar.gz</code> and its accompanying release files.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <h2>What this release does not establish</h2>
    <p>A passing verification result does not prove that:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the research project or its scientific conclusions are correct;</li>
      <li>input data or researcher declarations are true;</li>
      <li>cause-and-effect claims are justified;</li>
      <li>a paper will be accepted for publication;</li>
      <li>complete regulatory compliance has been established; or</li>
      <li>methods outside the explicitly supported Release 1 procedure are valid.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The verifier reports results for explicit checks and scopes rather than returning a
      single overall <code>VERIFIED</code> label. This Layer 1 release also does not, by
      itself, mean that the nomue application has shipped Release 1-backed product
      support.
    </p>
  </section>
</PublicationLayout>
